          body: `The patriarch of Genesis 12–25, called by God out of Ur and given the promise of a great nation through his son Isaac. Old, childless for decades, he and Sarah finally receive Isaac in their old age. Then, in Genesis 22, God commands him to take Isaac to the land of Moriah and offer him there as a burnt offering. Abraham rises early, saddles his ass, takes two servants and his only son, travels three days to the place God has shown, builds the altar, binds Isaac, takes the knife, raises it. The angel stops him at the last moment; a ram caught in a thicket is offered instead. Kierkegaard's reading is uncompromising: Abraham believed both that God required him to kill his son and that God would somehow give Isaac back, and he proceeded toward the killing on the strength of both beliefs simultaneously. This is, for Kierkegaard, what faith looks like — and it is what most contemporary Christians have stopped recognising as faith because it is too uncomfortable to acknowledge.`,
